word_freq.txt — provenance and license
=======================================

Contents
--------
`word_freq.txt` is a derived table mapping the top 30,000 alphabetic English
unigrams to a min-max-normalized log-frequency "rarity" value in [0, 1000]
(0 = most common, 1000 = rarest in the table). It is used by
`promptcomp.scoring.heuristic.HeuristicScorer` as a deterministic corpus-rarity
signal (a tf-idf-style word-importance weight, cf. Filippova & Strube 2008).

Source
------
Derived from Peter Norvig's `count_1w.txt` unigram frequency list:
    https://norvig.com/ngrams/

That list reports counts from the Google Web Trillion-Word Corpus, published by
Norvig alongside the book chapter "Natural Language Corpus Data" (in *Beautiful
Data*, O'Reilly, 2009). The underlying token counts are from Google's
Web 1T 5-gram corpus.

Derivation
----------
Only the raw integer counts were used. For the top 30,000 purely-alphabetic
lowercase words by frequency, rarity was computed as the min-max-normalized
negative log-frequency:

    rarity(w) = (log(count_max) - log(count(w))) / (log(count_max) - log(count_min))

and stored as `round(rarity * 1000)`. The corpus total cancels under min-max
normalization, so no absolute frequencies are reproduced here — only relative
rarity ranks. The runtime performs no logarithms.

Use
---
The counts are published by Norvig for free use. This derived table redistributes
only normalized relative-rarity ranks (not the original counts) for the internal
scoring heuristic. If you require a differently-licensed frequency source, the
generator can be re-pointed at any unigram list; see the project notes.
